{
  "headline": "Syria's Foreign Ministry condemns Houthi drone attack on Medina power station",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Syria’s Foreign Ministry issued a condemnation on Friday regarding a Houthi drone attack on a power distribution station located in Medina, Saudi Arabia. According to the North Press Agency, the ministry stated that the strike targeted civilian infrastructure, specifically a station that supplies electricity to the Prophet’s Mosque. The ministry further asserted that the attack violated Saudi Arabia’s sovereignty and threatened regional stability."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Gabby Otchere-Darko states Akufo-Addo is allowing Bawumia space to assert leadership of NPP",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Gabby Asare Otchere-Darko, a leading member of the New Patriotic Party (NPP), has stated that former President Nana Addo Dankwa Akufo-Addo is providing the party’s flagbearer, Dr. Mahamudu Bawumia, the space to establish himself as the party's leader. According to Otchere-Darko, Dr. Bawumia now occupies the NPP leadership position and commands the support of the party. He described the relationship between the former president and Dr. Bawumia, who served as Vice President from 2017 to 2025, as “excellent,” dismissing suggestions of tension between the two. Otchere-Darko noted that political dynamics changed following the 2024 presidential election, during which Dr. Bawumia contested the presidency as the NPP candidate after serving as Vice President under Mr. Akufo-Addo. He observed that the former president did not publicly endorse Dr. Bawumia during the party’s 2024 presidential primary."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "G7 to release 100 million barrels of oil and diesel reserves",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Group of Seven (G7) has agreed to a coordinated release of 100 million barrels of crude oil and diesel from emergency reserves over a four-month period. Following an emergency meeting convened via video conference by French President Emmanuel Macron, the group—which includes Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, the United Kingdom, the United States, and the European Union—announced the plan through the International Energy Agency (IEA). The release is scheduled to begin immediately and will include a substantial diesel release within the first 20 days. It remains unclear how many oil and diesel stocks each individual member will contribute to the total.  According to NPR, the decision followed increased pressure from Washington, as President Donald Trump had threatened to stop all U.S. diesel fuel exports if European nations did not release their own stockpiles. Al Jazeera reports that the G7's decision comes amid soaring global energy prices attributed to the wars in Ukraine and between the U.S., Israel, and Iran. Hamad Hussain, a climate and commodities economist at Capital Economics, told Al Jazeera that while the release may put downward pressure on global diesel prices, the impact could be short-lived as it serves as a temporary solution to a supply crunch. Africanews noted that following the announcement, international benchmark Brent crude briefly fell below $100 per barrel before stabilizing around $102, while U.S. oil prices fell by up to five percent."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Stakeholders call for National Political History and Heritage Centre in Saltpond",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Ernest De-Graft Egyir, President of the Mfantseman-Saltpond Development Alliance (MSDA), has called for the establishment of a National Political History and Heritage Centre at Saltpond in Ghana. Speaking at a Founders’ Day commemoration themed “Saltpond Heritage, Ghana’s Future, Turning History into Opportunity,” Egyir proposed that the center be used to preserve the country's political history and transform local historic sites into opportunities for tourism, research, education, and economic development. He suggested the institution should document Ghana's transition from colonial rule to independence by preserving political archives, oral histories, photographs, and other records. Egyir stated the center should function as a national institution for civic education and remembrance rather than a conventional museum, and should be linked to a wider heritage corridor involving hospitality and local enterprise. He emphasized that the initiative should be national and non-partisan, aiming to tell Ghana's story comprehensively. To achieve this, he called for support from the government, the Ministry of Tourism, Culture and Creative Arts, the Ghana Tourism Authority, traditional authorities, universities, historians, the private sector, development partners, and the diaspora. Egyir identified Canaan Lodge as a potential landmark for the development, noting that historical records confirm the United Gold Coast Convention (UGCC) was launched there on August 4, 1947."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Israeli settlers and forces attack Palestinians and journalists in West Bank",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Al Jazeera reported that on a Saturday, Israeli settlers protected by the Israeli army attacked olive pickers, burned olive trees, and detained dozens of people across the occupied West Bank. According to Al Jazeera's colleagues on the ground, these actions took place at olive groves in the village of Abu Dis, the town of Awarta, and the az-Zahra area in the town of Beita. An anonymous activist volunteering with Palestinian communities reported that approximately 50 masked settlers approached an olive harvest, threw stones, and attacked a house, after which soldiers arrived, held Palestinians and activists at gunpoint, and forced them to leave the area. Al Jazeera footage also captured settlers smashing a Palestinian vehicle in Osarin and armed settlers attacking a Palestinian home in Beita. In the village of Osarin, the Palestinian news agency Wafa reported that tear gas was fired at Palestinians and journalists, homes were attacked, vehicles were stoned, and several villagers were assaulted. Additionally, Al Jazeera reported that Israeli forces detained and assaulted journalists in the Jabal Qamass area of Beita, south of Nablus."
    },
    {
      "text": "Palestinian media reported that residents have attempted to repel settler attacks, which they described as part of a wider pogrom against Palestinians in the West Bank that has intensified since February. Since October 2023, Israelis have killed 1,212 Palestinians in the West Bank, including 254 children. According to the reporting, most of these killings were carried out by soldiers, while more than a quarter were committed by settlers."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ghana Library Authority signs MoU with AfCFTA Young Entrepreneurs Federation for Mahama Presidential Library",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Ghana Library Authority (GhLA) signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) with the AfCFTA Young Entrepreneurs Federation (AfYEF) in Accra on October 2, 2026, to support the development of the John Dramani Mahama Presidential Library and Legacy Centre (JDM-PLLC). The project, which has been under development for more than three years, is being established in honor of President John Dramani Mahama. Under the terms of the agreement, the GhLA will provide institutional expertise in libraries, archives, and knowledge development to assist in shaping the research and archival functions of the proposed institution. AfYEF, the initiator of the project, will lead broader development efforts, including the mobilization of resources and partnerships for the center's establishment. GhLA Executive Director Ziblim Alhassan Betintiche stated that the JDM-PLLC could become the country's first fully functioning presidential library, noting that the existing Kwame Nkrumah Memorial facility does not operate as a conventional presidential library."
    },
    {
      "text": "The MoU establishes a framework for the national launch of the project, scheduled for November 30, 2026, at the University for Development Studies (UDS) in Tamale. This launch is intended to present the project's development journey and initiate a phase involving master planning, fundraising, phased implementation, and the establishment of founding partnerships. The proposed institutional and knowledge hub is planned for Tamale, while Busunu and Bole have been proposed as complementary locations for heritage, leadership, community, and tourism."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Hundreds mourn 105 people in Gaza City vigil after bodies recovered from rubble",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Hundreds of mourners held a vigil in Gaza City on September 30, 2026, for 105 people killed during Israel's war on the Palestinian territory, according to reporting by Africanews. The bodies were recovered over the previous several days from the rubble of buildings destroyed by Israeli strikes during the nearly three-year conflict. The remains were wrapped in white bags and draped with Palestinian flags, with attendees gathering around the bodies, some of which were piled atop the rubble where they were originally found. Lighting for the vigil was provided by plastic candles and red lights from ambulance trucks. Fawaz Ziyara, who attended to mourn relatives, stated the victims were targeted at the beginning of the war on October 25, 2023, and remained buried due to the difficulty of reaching them. Taghreed Hinnawi, the mother of a victim, said the recovery allowed families to see and bid farewell to the deceased."
    },
    {
      "text": "Africanews reports that mass funerals have become more frequent in Gaza, noting that Israel has not allowed the heavy machinery required to clear rubble and recover the dead into the territory. On September 29, 2026, mourners in central Gaza's Nuseirat refugee camp held a mass funeral for nearly 50 people killed during the war. Earlier this month, the United Nations estimated that more than 8,000 bodies remain buried under rubble. According to the Gaza Ministry of Health, more than 74,000 Palestinians have been killed since October 7, 2023."
    }
  ]
}

