{"headline": "Russian strike hits Kyiv's Northern Bridge" , "pages": 
[
  {
    "text": "A Russian strike hit the Pivnichnyi (Northern) Bridge in Kyiv on October 1, 2026, according to Mayor Vitali Klitschko. The attack, which followed a drone alert, damaged the bridge's road surface and overhead trolleybus wires, injuring two people. Klitschko stated that traffic from the left bank to the right bank was blocked following the strike. This incident marks the second major bridge in the capital to be targeted by Russia in two days, following multiple drone strikes on the Pivdennyi (Southern) Bridge earlier in the week. Russia stated its drones hit the two bridges because they are used to transfer Ukrainian troops and military cargo. A total of eight bridges connect Kyiv's western and eastern banks across the Dnipro River. The strikes have contributed to traffic congestion on the city's major roads. On October 2, 2026, gridlock was reported in the city, though not at the same level as the previous day. The Russian Foreign Ministry issued a statement on Saturday urging foreign diplomats and nationals to leave Kyiv, stating that individuals are exposing themselves to mortal danger and that the Russian Armed Forces will continue to deliver massive retaliatory strikes."
  }
]
}

{
  "headline": "NPP National Delegates Conference held in Kumasi to elect national executives",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "More than 7,000 delegates of the New Patriotic Party (NPP) gathered at the Baba Yara Sports Stadium in Kumasi for the party’s National Delegates Congress. The all-day event, held on Saturday, forms part of the NPP’s preparations toward the 2028 general elections. The congress was structured in two parts, with delegates expected to consider proposed amendments to portions of the party’s constitution before proceeding to the election of national officers, which was expected to be the main focus of the congress. A total of 7,163 delegates were expected to participate in the exercise. Regional delegate counts included 1,122 from the Ashanti Region, 773 from Greater Accra, and 732 from the Eastern Region, while the Western North region had 223 and the Savannah Region had 184 delegates."
    },
    {
      "text": "Acting National Chairman of the NPP, Danquah Smith Buttey, attributed a delay in the commencement of voting at the conference to the late arrival of delegates. Speaking at the Baba Yara Sports Stadium on Saturday, Mr. Buttey stated that voting would begin once the required number of delegates had arrived, noting, “We are waiting for the delegates. They are not here. So, if 1/3 of them comes then we start.” When asked for a specific reason for the delay, Mr. Buttey stated he did not know and noted that delegates were still at their various locations. He dismissed allegations that candidates were still engaging delegates on the day of the election, describing such engagements as a feature of the party’s internal elections."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Co-pilot attack on flydubai flight leads to emergency landing in Saudi Arabia",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "On September 30, 2026, at approximately 9:30 a.m. local time, a flydubai Boeing 737 flying from Dubai to Tel Aviv experienced an attack in the cockpit involving the co-pilot. According to witness testimony provided to Israel’s Channel 12 TV, passenger Tali Manes reported hearing heavy blows against the cockpit door before the co-pilot opened it. Inside, witnesses observed a struggle between the co-pilot, who was armed with a sharp weapon, and the badly injured pilot. At the time of the incident, no one was at the controls of the aircraft. Following the struggle, the plane entered a steep dive, losing 16,000 feet of altitude in approximately one minute. A group of passengers rushed to the cockpit and overpowered the co-pilot. A plumber among the passengers attempted to pull the aircraft out of its dive, while two off-duty pilots on board took control of the nearly rudderless aircraft. The flight, carrying 182 passengers, eventually landed in Tabuk, Saudi Arabia, 90 minutes later. While details of the event remain unclear, experts have expressed skepticism that the co-pilot's only motivation was suicide, suggesting the question of radicalization is central to the investigation."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Victoria Bright calls for investigation into organized crime networks behind Ghana-linked drug trade",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "International corporate lawyer and entrepreneur Victoria Bright has called for Ghana to investigate the organized crime networks facilitating narcotics trafficking through the country, rather than focusing solely on individual drug seizures. Speaking on JoyNews’ Newsfile, Bright stated that the frequency and scale of recent seizures suggest Ghana's involvement in international drug trafficking may extend beyond isolated incidents and should be treated as a national security and institutional integrity concern. She questioned how trafficking networks consistently move drugs through ports and airports and whether they have gained influence within state institutions. Bright noted that consignments have been intercepted via commercial containers, road transport, and passenger aviation, including methods such as hiding drugs beneath sand in cement trucks, inside wood shipments, and in cassava flour."
    },
    {
      "text": "Bright cited figures from a German research analyst indicating that 28 drug busts were recorded between March 2025 and September 2026, 11 of which involved cocaine. According to these figures, approximately 16 tonnes of Ghana-linked cocaine, with an estimated street value of $1.3 billion to $1.7 billion, were intercepted during that period. Of the 11 cocaine seizures, seven occurred in Ghana and four were intercepted abroad. Bright noted that approximately 3.6 tonnes were intercepted in Ghana compared to about 4.4 tonnes intercepted in Europe, raising questions regarding the amount of drugs passing through the country undetected. She highlighted the significance of these figures by noting that Ghana does not produce cocaine."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Freddie Blay alleges Mahama ordered demolition of son’s Polo Beach Club",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Former Chairman of the New Patriotic Party (NPP), Freddie Blay, has accused President John Dramani Mahama of personally directing the demolition of his son’s Polo Beach Club in the La Beach enclave of Accra. Speaking during the NPP’s National Delegates Congress on Saturday, October 3, Blay stated that the demolition, which occurred on October 2, 2026, during a security-backed clearance operation, was an issue that should be raised against the government. Blay alleged that the President ordered the destruction of the development despite an ongoing court dispute regarding the property. He claimed the President has no respect for the law, questioning the involvement of the President and National Security while the ownership dispute remained before the court without a final determination."
    },
    {
      "text": "Blay argued the demolition was inappropriate because the court has yet to determine the ownership of the property, a dispute involving his son’s business and the Labadi Beach Hotel that has reportedly been before the court for approximately two to three years. Blay rejected claims that the Polo Beach Club was operating as a squatter on land belonging to the hotel, asserting that his son acquired land from the La Stool and conducted business on land that originally belonged to the La Stool. He stated that the land has been leased out and that legal processes are ongoing."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Frank Annoh-Dompreh states NPP requires leaders with a “war-like mindset” to challenge government",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Speaking on the sidelines of the New Patriotic Party (NPP) National Delegates Conference in Kumasi on Saturday, Minority Chief Whip Frank Annoh-Dompreh stated that the party requires national leaders possessing a “war-like mindset” to complement the efforts of the parliamentary caucus. Annoh-Dompreh said the parliamentary caucus cannot carry out its responsibilities alone and expressed a need for incoming national executives to work closely with the Minority in Parliament and the party’s flagbearer to tackle national issues and hold the government to account. He noted that the party expects a new group of leaders prepared to confront national challenges and intensify scrutiny of the government through political and parliamentary action, including stronger oversight of government deliverables. Annoh-Dompreh clarified that his use of the term “war-like” was not a call for violence or taking up arms, but rather a reference to being determined to do battle regarding national issues and executing oversight."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "MT Asharami Ghana arrives in Tema as government emphasizes LPG infrastructure investment",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The MT Asharami Ghana, a 40,000-cubic-metre liquefied petroleum gas (LPG) carrier with a cargo capacity of 5,000 metric tonnes, has arrived in Tema. During a reception ceremony for the vessel, Ghana's Minister for Energy and Green Transition, Dr. John Abdulai Jinapor, stated that strategic investments in LPG infrastructure are necessary to strengthen the country's energy security and expand access to cleaner energy. Dr. Jinapor noted that such investments are essential as Ghana seeks to improve LPG availability for businesses and households while positioning itself as a regional energy and logistics hub. He characterized the arrival of the vessel as a step toward expanding cleaner energy solutions for Ghanaians and highlighted the importance of collaboration between the government and private-sector players in developing energy infrastructure. Dr. Jinapor also commended Sahara Group for its contributions to the Ghanaian energy sector. The vessel was purpose-built to serve Ghana and neighboring markets and is expected to support LPG supply and energy security across West Africa. Wale Ajibade, Executive Director of Sahara Group, described the vessel's maiden voyage to Ghana as a strategic investment in regional energy infrastructure covering shipping, storage, and downstream distribution. Ajibade stated the investment reflects the group's confidence in Ghana's potential and its commitment to providing sustainable energy solutions."
    }
  ]
}
